==>     subnetting:

192.168.1.10

subnet tells us which part of the IP is the host and which part is the network!
network == your neighboerhood 
host == your house address


When an octet in the subnet mask is 255, that octet belongs ENTIRELY to the Network ID.
When an octet in the subnet mask is 0, that octet belongs ENTIRELY to the Host portion.


IP Address:     192.168.1.50    ← This is the FULL IP address
Subnet Mask:    255.255.255.0   ← This tells us what's network vs host

Network ID:     192.168.1.0     ← Network part
Host portion:   .50             ← Host part (the "rest" of the IP)


==> in short:


IP address = Network part + Host part
Mask 255 → that octet belongs to the network
Mask 0 → that octet belongs to the host
/24 = last octet is for hosts

for /24 network | e.g., 192.168.1.0/24


192.168.1.0	Network ID (all host bits = 0)	    RESERVED
192.168.1.1 → 192.168.1.254	Valid hosts	        Can use
192.168.1.255	Broadcast (all host bits = 1)	RESERVED


why do we .0 for the network in /24?
The .0 doesn't "belong" to the network in the way the first 3 octets do.


